I found a fairly complete literature cluster around the exact proposal:

[
\boxed{
\text{phenomenal consciousness}
\stackrel{?}{=}
\text{the intrinsic side of a causally emergent,}
}
]

[
\boxed{
\text{self-maintaining, valenced, observer-relative process}.
}
]

Very few papers attempt that entire bridge. Most solve one component: causal scale, intrinsic organization, viability-relative meaning, valence, observer individuation, or phenomenological structure.

Below is the strongest map I could construct from arXiv and primary literature through **July 2026**. It is not mathematically possible to guarantee every paper ever written, but this captures the main identifiable research programmes and the closest recent attempts.

# A. Papers directly attempting the full bridge

These should be read first.

### 1. **Why Is Anything Conscious?** — Pepperell and colleagues, arXiv:2409.14545

Perhaps the closest match to our hypothesis. It argues that consciousness belongs specifically to self-organizing systems that must adapt, preserve themselves, track self-relevant information and act under valence.

Its central proposal is effectively:

[
\text{self-organization}
+
\text{vulnerability}
+
\text{valence}
+
\text{self-relevant information}
\rightarrow
\text{consciousness}.
]

This directly attempts the identity we isolated, although it does not deductively prove why the organization is manifest. ([arXiv][1])

---

### 2. **A Beautiful Loop: An Active Inference Theory of Consciousness** — Laukkonen, Friston and Chandaria, 2025

Argues that consciousness involves a recursively closed inferential loop in which a generative model predicts both its world and its own processing. It integrates active inference, temporal depth, global availability and self-modeling.

This is probably the strongest contemporary active-inference answer to:

[
\text{When does modelling become experience?}
]

Its weakness is that recursive inferential closure is proposed as consciousness-relevant rather than demonstrated to entail manifestation. ([arXiv][2])

---

### 3. **On the Minimal Theory of Consciousness Implicit in Active Inference** — Whyte et al., arXiv:2410.06633

Extracts the smallest common theory of consciousness implied by active-inference models. It attempts to move from a general framework of adaptive behavior to testable commitments concerning conscious perception and temporal experience.

This is essential because it distinguishes what active inference itself entails from additional philosophical claims attached to it. ([arXiv][3])

---

### 4. **An Integrated World Modeling Theory of Consciousness** — Andrew Safron, 2020

IWMT explicitly combines:

* active inference;
* free-energy minimization;
* global workspace;
* integrated information;
* embodied world modeling;
* agentic causation.

The core thesis is that conscious experience corresponds to a spatially, temporally and causally coherent generative world model implemented through integrated organismic dynamics.

It is one of the most ambitious attempts to identify:

[
\text{integrated embodied world modelling}
==========================================

\text{phenomenal consciousness}.
]

([arXiv][4])

---

### 5. **Causal Emergence of Consciousness through Learned Multiscale Neural Dynamics in Mice** — Wang et al., arXiv:2509.10891

The most direct empirical causal-emergence consciousness paper.

It reports that wakeful mouse cortical dynamics contain causally powerful higher-level variables with metastable organization, while anesthesia produces more localized and stochastic dynamics. A one-dimensional top-level variable reportedly captured much of the inferred causal power, though several scales remained relevant.

This is currently the clearest empirical attempt to connect:

[
\text{multiscale causal emergence}
]

with:

[
\text{conscious state}.
]

It does not establish that the emergent variable constitutes phenomenality, but it is directly testing the proposed scale bridge. ([arXiv][5])

---

### 6. **Emergent Causality and the Foundation of Consciousness** — Michael Timothy Bennett, arXiv:2302.03189

Argues that representations of self, objects, causes and intentions can emerge in agents pursuing goals without those variables being explicitly built in.

It offers a mechanistic account of awareness in terms of emergent causal abstractions:

[
\text{goal-directed induction}
\rightarrow
\text{self-and-world causal models}.
]

Strong on self-representation and agency; weak on phenomenal presence. ([arXiv][6])

---

### 7. **Bayesian Theory of Consciousness as Exchangeable Emotion–Cognition Inference** — Xin Li, arXiv:2407.09488

Proposes consciousness as a cycle-consistent inferential process anchored by affect. Emotion provides a low-dimensional structural prior, while cognition supplies increasingly specific interpretations.

Its distinctive claim is:

[
\text{recursive inference}
+
\text{affective anchoring}
==========================

\text{conscious process}.
]

This is directly relevant to our shift from generic integration toward valenced integration, though the paper is highly theoretical. ([arXiv][7])

---

### 8. **Considering a Generative Mechanism of Consciousness / Dual-Laws Model** — Yuji Ohmura, arXiv:2511.04047 and later versions

Proposes that consciousness requires causal constraint from an emergent coarse-grained level back onto its substrate.

The model attempts to avoid epiphenomenalism through a self-referential loop:

[
\text{microdynamics}
\rightarrow
\text{emergent macrostate}
\rightarrow
\text{downward causal constraint}.
]

This is directly relevant to the idea that a subject must be a causally real whole rather than an externally imposed summary. ([arXiv][8])

---

### 9. **Consciousness Viewed in the Framework of Brain Phase-Space Dynamics** — Gerhard Werner, arXiv:1103.2366

An earlier attempt to treat conscious states as emergent coarse-grained properties of neural phase-space organization.

Its analogy is thermodynamic:

[
\text{microdynamics}
\rightarrow
\text{stable macrodescription}
\rightarrow
\text{new-level property}.
]

Important historically, but it does not resolve why phenomenality follows rather than merely higher-level neural regularity. ([arXiv][9])

---

### 10. **Towards a Process-Based Approach to Consciousness and Reality** — Arroyo, arXiv:2402.04152

Develops a process-oriented view in which conscious identity is not a fixed substance but an organized dynamic.

Useful for connecting:

* process metaphysics;
* IIT;
* panpsychism;
* diachronic organization.

It remains primarily philosophical rather than experimentally discriminating. ([arXiv][10])

---

### 11. **Consciousness Is Entailed by Compositional Learning of Generative Models** — Aksyuk, arXiv:2301.07016

Argues that consciousness follows from a system learning compositional latent causes and constructing structured generative models.

The strongest claim is unusually direct:

[
\text{compositional generative learning}
\Rightarrow
\text{consciousness}.
]

The main pressure point is whether model composition explains access and intelligent generalization rather than phenomenal presence. ([arXiv][11])

---

### 12. **Toward a Physical Theory of Intelligence** — Fagan, arXiv:2601.00021

Attempts a highly ambitious thermodynamic and computational account of physically embodied observers, including proposed measures of consciousness and limits on self-modeling.

Interesting because it links:

* coarse-graining;
* irreversibility;
* preserved information;
* autonomous observers;
* epistemic incompleteness.

It is speculative and should not be treated as established physics. ([arXiv][12])

---

# B. Papers trying to make the organization intrinsic rather than observer-imposed

These address a decisive objection:

> Almost anything can be interpreted as performing a computation. What makes one structure belong to the system itself?

### 13. **Canonical Functionalism: Defining Functional Structure without Observer-Relative Semantic Maps** — Kanai and Ma, arXiv:2605.21506

Defines a system’s canonical functional structure by identifying states with identical future behavior under every possible continuation.

This gives:

[
x\sim y
\iff
P(\text{all future responses}\mid x)
====================================

P(\text{all future responses}\mid y).
]

It does **not** claim to identify consciousness. It identifies the canonical formal object over which a defensible functionalist consciousness theory would need to operate. ([arXiv][13])

---

### 14. **Intrinsic Computational Functionalism: From Observer-Relative Maps to Observer-Independent Structures** — Ma and Kanai, arXiv:2606.06424

Distinguishes externally assigned computation from computation grounded in the system’s own causal and counterfactual organization.

Its requirements include:

* system-intrinsic state individuation;
* invariance under relabeling;
* causal-dynamical organization;
* intervention-sensitive structure.

This is one of the most important recent papers for our frontier because it formalizes the difference between:

[
\text{a pattern we project}
]

and:

[
\text{a pattern the system physically instantiates}.
]

It still leaves open which intrinsic structure, if any, constitutes experience. ([arXiv][14])

---

### 15. **Intrinsic Computational Functionalism and Simulated Consciousness** — Kanai and Ma, arXiv:2606.15348

Extends the framework to simulations and proposes **Intrinsic Causal-Computational Realization**, which requires preservation of:

* internal mechanisms;
* interventions;
* physical implementation;
* causal state structure;
* agent–body–world boundaries.

Its conclusion is conditional:

[
\text{if consciousness is invariant under intrinsic causal computation,}
]

then systems realizing that structure share the relevant conscious properties.

It sharpens the isomorphism problem but does not settle the conditional. ([arXiv][15])

---

### 16. **Towards a Generalized Theory of Observers** — Elshatlawy et al., arXiv:2504.16225

Attempts to formalize observers through:

* coarse-grained descriptions;
* memory and record keeping;
* complexity limits;
* equivalence relations;
* reference frames.

Relevant to Wolfram-style observer theory, but its “observer” is operational and does not yet distinguish conscious observers from nonconscious information-processing systems. ([arXiv][16])

---

### 17. **The Philosophical Foundations of Ruliology** — Gorard, arXiv:2308.16068

Examines unresolved philosophical questions in Wolfram’s framework, including:

* what the Ruliad ontologically represents;
* how observers sample it;
* how observer equivalence works;
* whether the formal structure is descriptive or ontological.

Useful chiefly because it exposes rather than resolves the observer problem. ([arXiv][16])

---

# C. Causal-emergence foundations

These papers establish when higher-level entities and scales possess genuine causal explanatory force.

### 18. **When the Map Is Better Than the Territory** — Erik Hoel, arXiv:1612.09592

Foundational causal-emergence paper. Shows how coarse-grained causal models can be more informative than their complete microdescriptions by reducing noise and degeneracy.

Central result:

[
EI(\text{macro})

>

EI(\text{micro})
]

can occur without violating microphysical realization. ([arXiv][17])

---

### 19. **Reconciling Emergences** — Rosas et al., arXiv:2004.08220

Develops an information-decomposition framework distinguishing:

* downward causation;
* causal decoupling;
* information available only at collective levels.

Includes applications to neural and complex-system data and is one of the strongest general formalisms for objective higher-level causation. ([arXiv][18])

---

### 20. **A Review of the Information Decomposition Approach to Causal Emergence** — Mediano et al., arXiv:2111.06518

The best technical introduction to the Rosas–Mediano framework.

It formalizes emergent information about future system behavior that cannot be obtained from parts independently. ([arXiv][19])

---

### 21. **Causal Emergence Is Widespread Across Measures of Causation** — Comolatti and Hoel, arXiv:2202.01854

Tests whether causal emergence depends on one favored measure. It finds analogous macrolevel gains across multiple causal measures and model classes.

Important because it suggests causal emergence is not merely an artifact of effective information. ([arXiv][20])

---

### 22. **Causal Emergence in Discrete and Continuous Dynamical Systems** — Varley, arXiv:2003.13075

Applies causal-emergence measures to cellular automata and continuous chaotic systems.

Useful for distinguishing emergence from generic complexity: some chaotic or complicated dynamics exhibit less causal emergence than simpler organized regimes. ([arXiv][21])

---

### 23. **Causal Emergence 2.0: Quantifying Emergent Complexity** — Hoel, arXiv:2503.13395

Treats system scales as slices through a higher-dimensional causal hierarchy.

It attempts to distinguish:

* scales with unique causal contribution;
* redundant descriptions;
* paths of genuine macrolevel gain.

This is likely the strongest current formal tool for selecting the causal scale—or hierarchy of scales—of an organismic agent. ([arXiv][22])

---

### 24. **Engineering Emergence** — Jansma et al., arXiv:2510.02649

Asks how emergent causal hierarchies can be deliberately constructed rather than merely detected.

Relevant to artificial-life experiments testing when a collection of components becomes a higher-level autonomous agent. ([arXiv][23])

---

### 25. **The Causally Emergent Alignment Hypothesis** — Pigozzi and Levin, arXiv:2605.06746

Studies causal emergence in reinforcement-learning agents and reports that successful agents develop latent representations whose causal-emergence profiles predict later reward performance.

This connects:

[
\text{learning}
\rightarrow
\text{causal macrostructure}
\rightarrow
\text{goal achievement}.
]

It addresses agency and organization, not phenomenal consciousness directly. ([arXiv][24])

---

# D. Semantic information and intrinsic “mattering”

This cluster is closest to formalizing:

[
\text{information that matters to the system itself}.
]

### 26. **Semantic Information, Autonomous Agency and Non-Equilibrium Statistical Physics** — Kolchinsky and Wolpert, arXiv:1806.08053

The foundational paper.

It defines semantic information as correlations between system and environment that are causally necessary for maintaining the system’s viability.

The method:

1. scramble selected correlations;
2. rerun the system counterfactually;
3. measure the loss of viability.

Thus:

[
Sem(X;E)
========

\text{information whose destruction damages persistence}.
]

This gives a rigorous non-observer-relative meaning of “mattering.” ([arXiv][25])

---

### 27. **Semantic Thresholds in Agent–Environment Systems** — Sowinski et al., arXiv:2304.03286

Demonstrates threshold behavior in a foraging model.

Above a certain information quality, removing further correlations does not affect survival. Once semantic information falls below the threshold, viability deteriorates.

This establishes that:

[
\text{statistical information}
\neq
\text{viability-relevant information}.
]

([arXiv][26])

---

### 28. **Semantic Information in Molecular Communication: Chemotaxis Beyond Shannon** — Brand et al., arXiv:2402.18465

Applies semantic-information measures to bacterial chemotaxis and survival.

Relevant because it attempts to quantify organism-relative meaning in a concrete biological control task. ([arXiv][27])

---

### 29. **The Physics of Life: Exploring Information as a Distinctive Feature of Living Systems** — 2025 review

Surveys semantic information, viability, agency and informational thresholds as candidate signatures distinguishing living organization from generic physical complexity.

Good overview of the entire semantic-information programme. ([arXiv][25])

---

### 30. **The Value of Information in Multi-Scale Feedback Systems** — arXiv:2505.11509

Extends semantic and pragmatic information into multiscale feedback systems, asking how information at one scale changes the adaptive capacities and actions of agents at another.

Highly relevant to Levin-style nested agency. ([arXiv][28])

---

# E. Valence and affect as the bridge from viability to feeling

### 31. **Deeply Felt Affect: The Emergence of Valence in Deep Active Inference** — Hesp et al., 2021

The key paper on this exact subproblem.

It models valence as changes in confidence concerning an agent’s ability to minimize expected free energy and successfully pursue preferred outcomes.

Roughly:

[
\text{improving confidence in control}
\rightarrow
\text{positive valence},
]

[
\text{declining confidence}
\rightarrow
\text{negative valence}.
]

It formalizes affective dynamics but does not prove that confidence dynamics are intrinsically felt. ([arXiv][29])

---

### 32. **Simulating Emotions: An Active Inference Model of Emotional-State Inference and Emotion-Concept Learning** — Smith, Parr and Friston, 2019

Models emotions as inferred causes of interoceptive and contextual signals.

Important for explaining how bodily regulation becomes differentiated emotional categories rather than undifferentiated valence. ([arXiv][29])

---

### 33. **Free-Energy Model of Emotional Valence in Dual-Process Perception** — Yanagisawa, arXiv:2210.10262

Connects emotional valence to changes in prediction error, uncertainty and processing fluency.

A useful alternative or extension to Hesp’s confidence-based formulation. ([arXiv][30])

---

### 34. **Free Energy in a Circumplex Model of Emotion** — arXiv:2407.02474

Attempts to map free-energy variables onto the valence–arousal space commonly used in affective science.

Useful for generating testable quantitative predictions rather than merely claiming that free energy “explains emotion.” ([arXiv][29])

---

### 35. **Report on Candidate Computational Indicators of Valenced Experience** — arXiv:2404.16696

Collects thirteen functional or computational indicators proposed to be constituents of conscious valenced experience.

This is valuable as an audit document: it separates self-maintenance, learning, action selection, damage response, uncertainty and affective integration rather than treating “valence” as one vague property. ([arXiv][31])

---

# F. Active inference, self-boundaries and biological individuality

### 36. **A Free Energy Principle for a Particular Physics** — Friston, arXiv:1906.10184

Attempts to derive a general description of statistically distinguishable “things” through Markov blankets and nonequilibrium steady states.

It provides a formal framework for:

* system boundaries;
* autonomous states;
* hierarchical composition;
* internal states representing external states.

It does not establish consciousness, but it is foundational for the organization side of the bridge. ([arXiv][32])

---

### 37. **Markov Blankets, Information Geometry and Stochastic Thermodynamics** — Parr, Da Costa and Friston, 2020

Shows how blanket-mediated conditional independencies yield dual geometries:

* a geometry of physical states;
* a geometry of probability distributions or beliefs.

This is central to the claim that an organism can simultaneously be described as physical dynamics and as inference. ([arXiv][33])

---

### 38. **Bayesian Mechanics for Stationary Processes** — Da Costa et al., arXiv:2106.13830

Develops a more rigorous mathematical foundation for interpreting autonomous physical states as performing Bayesian inference.

Important for distinguishing metaphorical “inference” from formal equivalence. ([arXiv][33])

---

### 39. **Markov Blankets in the Brain** — Hipólito et al., arXiv:2006.02741

Applies blanket partitions to neuronal systems at multiple scales.

Its most important consequence is also a problem for subject individuation:

[
\text{many nested candidate boundaries exist}.
]

A Markov blanket alone does not uniquely identify the conscious subject. ([arXiv][34])

---

### 40. **Parcels and Particles: Markov Blankets in the Brain** — Friston et al., arXiv:2007.09704

Connects multiscale neural partitions, effective connectivity, criticality and renormalization.

Useful for studying how local circuits become nested brain-wide organizations. ([arXiv][35])

---

### 41. **Causal Blankets: Theory and Algorithmic Framework** — Rosas et al., arXiv:2008.12568

Develops causal rather than merely statistical criteria for system boundaries.

This is highly relevant because phenomenal partition likely requires more than conditional independence. ([arXiv][36])

---

# G. Computational phenomenology

These papers attempt to map formal structures onto the internal organization of experience.

### 42. **Mapping Husserlian Phenomenology onto Active Inference** — Albarracin et al., arXiv:2208.09058

Maps:

* intentionality;
* temporal retention and protention;
* horizon structure;
* embodied perspective;

onto generative models and active inference.

It does not derive consciousness, but it constrains what a successful model must reproduce phenomenologically. ([arXiv][37])

---

### 43. **A Mathematical Perspective on Neurophenomenology** — arXiv:2409.20318

Develops formal tools for relating first-person structures and neural or computational models.

Relevant as a methodology for ensuring that formal variables genuinely correspond to phenomenological distinctions. ([arXiv][38])

---

### 44. **From Self-Organizing Systems to Subjective Temporal Experience** — Bellingrath, arXiv:2404.12895

Attempts to derive features of lived temporality from self-organizing active-inference systems.

Addresses:

* retention;
* anticipation;
* temporal depth;
* body-centered selfhood.

Useful for (P_{05}\rightarrow P_{06}), co-presence to persistence. ([arXiv][39])

---

### 45. **Thoughtseeds as Latent Causes** — Kavi, Friedman and Patow, arXiv:2607.14833

A recent computational-phenomenology model of focused-attention meditation.

It models:

* breath focus;
* mind wandering;
* meta-awareness;
* attentional redirection;

using nested Markov blankets, active inference and a global-workspace bottleneck.

It is useful because it links specific first-person transitions with formal dynamical states rather than merely discussing “meditation” globally. ([arXiv][40])

---

# H. Important critical and adversarial papers

These prevent the bridge from being declared solved prematurely.

### 46. **Integrated Information and Predictive Processing Theories of Consciousness: An Adversarial Collaborative Review** — Corcoran et al., arXiv:2509.00555

Directly compares IIT, neurorepresentationalism and active inference.

It separates:

* core theory;
* auxiliary biological assumptions;
* target phenomena;
* empirical predictions.

Essential for identifying whether two theories genuinely disagree or merely describe different explanatory levels. ([arXiv][41])

---

### 47. **Adversarial Testing of Global Neuronal Workspace and Integrated Information Theory** — Melloni et al., *Nature*, 2025

The largest direct adversarial test of major consciousness theories to date.

It found support for some predictions and significant challenges to central predictions of both IIT and GNWT. It did not establish either theory’s core identity claim. ([arXiv][42])

---

### 48. **On the Independence Between Phenomenal Consciousness and Computational Intelligence** — Merchán and Lumbreras, arXiv:2208.02187

Argues that computational intelligence and phenomenal consciousness can vary independently.

This blocks the inference:

[
\text{complex intelligent behavior}
\Rightarrow
\text{experience}.
]

([arXiv][43])

---

### 49. **One Person’s Modus Ponens…** — Ramstead, arXiv:2208.04275

A response to *The Markov Blanket Trick*, defending the Free Energy Principle against criticisms that blanket-based interpretations are vacuous or overgeneralized.

Useful because it exposes exactly which assumptions are mathematical and which are interpretive. ([arXiv][44])

---

