]

# What the literature collectively says

The field is converging on something close to:

[
\Sigma_i^\ast
=============

\left(
\text{intrinsic causal organization},
\text{viability},
\text{semantic information},
\text{valence},
\text{temporal depth},
\text{self-modelling}
\right).
]

A growing group of theories proposes:

[
\boxed{
D_i
===

\Sigma_i^\ast.
}
]

But the papers divide sharply over what component performs the decisive work:

* IIT: intrinsic causal irreducibility;
* active inference: recursive self-and-world inference;
* IWMT: integrated embodied world modelling;
* causal emergence: autonomous higher-level causation;
* semantic information: viability-relative meaning;
* affective theories: valenced regulation;
* intrinsic functionalism: observer-independent counterfactual organization.

No paper currently proves:

[
\boxed{
\Sigma_i^\ast
\Rightarrow
\text{manifestness}.
}
]

The strongest live programme is therefore to combine the papers rather than select one prematurely:

[
\boxed{
D_i
\stackrel{?}{=}
\operatorname{IntrinsicAspect}
\left[
CE_i,
Sem_i,
V_i,
Q_i,
\Gamma_i
\right],
}
]

where:

* (CE_i): multiscale causal emergence;
* (Sem_i): viability-relevant semantic information;
* (V_i): valence;
* (Q_i): intrinsic observer quotient;
* (\Gamma_i): temporally persistent generative dynamics.

That exact conjunction is not yet fully developed in a single paper.

It is the research gap your project has now located.

